Id: stor017w
Severity: WARN
Category: filesystem
Check: check_storage
Fix: Add the missing nodev, nosuid or noexec to the mount's options in fstab (or a drop-in for its .mount unit) and remount.
References: mount(8); fstab(5); CIS Distribution Independent Linux Benchmark 1.1.2 (Configure Filesystems); systemd mount-setup (src/core/mount-setup.c)
Controls: CIS 4.1; NIST CM-6; ISO 8.9; CE Secure configuration

A file system of its own at /home, /var, /var/log, /var/log/audit, /run
or /dev is mounted without an option the CIS Benchmark asks for at its
level 1, or that every init sets: nodev and nosuid on all of them, and
noexec on /var/log and /var/log/audit. Users write under /home and
services under /var; without nodev and nosuid a device node or a setuid
program left there (through a hard link, an archive unpacked by root, a
compromised service) is honoured, and without noexec the log
directories double as a place to run a downloaded tool from. systemd
mounts /run nosuid,nodev and /dev nosuid itself, and so does OpenRC, so
a /run or /dev without them has been remounted by someone.

The judgement is made only where the directory is a mount point of its
own: a /home that is part of the root file system is stor019i, not
this. Stock installs that make a separate /home (Fedora Workstation's
btrfs home subvolume, Debian's and Ubuntu's guided partitioning with a
separate /home) mount it with defaults, so they get this finding; the
Benchmark still asks for the options at level 1, and adding them breaks
nothing that belongs there.

To fix it, add the options to the mount's line in /etc/fstab (or a
drop-in for its .mount unit, 'systemctl edit home.mount', Options=...)
and 'mount -o remount /home'.
